<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  One file, three accounts. Stmt is 1..n: a combined export carries every account the
  customer holds at that bank, each with its own IBAN, currency, balances and entries.

  Swiss Payment Standards *typically* delivers one account per file, which is precisely
  what makes a single-statement parser dangerous - it works until the bank sends this.

    Stmt 1  CH2100210210108311400  CHF  OPBD  1'000.00 + 200.00          = CLBD  1'200.00
    Stmt 2  CH5604835012345678009  CHF  OPBD     50.00 - 300.00          = CLBD   -250.00 (DBIT)
    Stmt 3  CH9300762011623852957  EUR  PRCD    400.00 - 100.00          = CLBD    300.00

  Stmt 2 closes with CdtDbtInd DBIT - an overdrawn account - so the closing balance is
  negative from the account holder's point of view.
  Stmt 3 opens with PRCD instead of OPBD, which some banks send; it is the previous
  closing booked balance and therefore the same number.
-->
